// Sample input to test this agent alone (the orchestrator sends the same shape).
return [{ json: {
  claim: { claim_id: 'CLM-TEST-RAG', description: 'Rear-ended at a red light. Rear bumper and boot lid damaged.' },
  policy: { policy_id: 'POL-1001', product: 'motor_comprehensive' },
  visual: { damaged_parts: ['pare-choc arriere', 'coffre'], pre_existing_damage_signs: false },
  invoice: { line_items: [ { part: 'Rear bumper', type: 'part' }, { part: 'Boot lid', type: 'part' }, { part: 'Labour', type: 'labor' } ] },
} }];

// Search query = accident description + damaged / invoiced parts; the product filters the clauses.
const j = $input.first().json;
const claim = j.claim || {};
const product = String(j.policy?.product || j.product_id || 'motor_comprehensive');
const seen = j.visual && !j.visual.error ? (j.visual.damaged_parts || []) : [];
const invoiced = j.invoice && !j.invoice.error
  ? (j.invoice.line_items || []).filter((l) => l.type === 'part').map((l) => l.part) : [];
const parts = [...new Set([...seen, ...invoiced].map(String))];
// the product is part of the query so the clause defining what this product covers is retrieved too
const productText = product === 'motor_third_party'
  ? 'Product motor_third_party: liability to third parties only, damage to the insured vehicle'
  : `Product ${product}: damage to the insured vehicle`;
const query = [productText, claim.description, parts.length ? `Damaged parts: ${parts.join(', ')}` : '', 'cover, exclusions and conditions']
  .filter(Boolean).join('. ').slice(0, 1200);
return [{ json: {
  claim_id: claim.claim_id, product, query, parts, description: String(claim.description || ''),
  pre_existing: j.visual?.pre_existing_damage_signs === true,
} }];

// The retrieved clauses, de-duplicated, become the only context the model may use.
const q = $('Build Query').first().json;
const clauses = [];
for (const d of $input.all().map((i) => i.json).filter((x) => x.document)) {
  const m = d.document.metadata || {};
  const id = m.clause_id || (String(d.document.pageContent).match(/C-\d+\.\d+/) || [])[0];
  if (!id || clauses.some((c) => c.clause_id === id)) continue;
  clauses.push({ clause_id: id, title: m.title || '', product_scope: m.product_scope || 'all',
                 score: Math.round((d.score ?? 0) * 1000) / 1000, text: d.document.pageContent });
}
return [{ json: {
  ...q, clauses, retrieved_ids: clauses.map((c) => c.clause_id),
  context: clauses.map((c) => `[${c.clause_id}] ${c.text}`).join('\n\n'),
} }];

parameters.text:
=You are the Policy RAG Agent of ClaimShield AI. Decide whether the damage in this motor insurance claim is covered by the customer's policy, using ONLY the policy clauses below.

Customer product: {{ $json.product }}
Accident description: {{ $json.description }}
Damaged / invoiced parts: {{ $json.parts.join(', ') || 'not available' }}
Photo analysis found signs of pre-existing damage: {{ $json.pre_existing }}

POLICY CLAUSES (retrieved from the policy wording):
{{ $json.context || 'No clause was retrieved.' }}

Rules:
1. coverage_status is COVERED only if a clause above covers this kind of damage for this product and no exclusion above applies.
2. EXCLUDED if a clause above clearly excludes it (for example damage to the insured vehicle under motor_third_party, or pre-existing damage).
3. Otherwise, or if the clauses are not enough to decide, REQUIRES_REVIEW.
4. cited_clauses: only IDs written in square brackets above, such as "C-2.1". Never cite a clause that is not above.
5. Do not judge fraud and do not compute amounts: other agents do that.

Return ONLY a raw JSON object, with no markdown and no text around it:
{"coverage_status": "COVERED or EXCLUDED or REQUIRES_REVIEW", "cited_clauses": ["C-x.y"], "reasoning": "one or two sentences citing the clauses", "flagged_exclusions": ["title of each exclusion that applies"]}

